Add database schema, row level security, and the ranking engine

Thirteen migrations covering the full data model, plus the ranking RPCs. All of
it runs in CI against real Postgres compiled to WebAssembly, so there is no
Docker dependency and no separate test schema to drift from the migrations.

The ranking engine is the component most likely to corrupt data in ways nobody
notices for weeks, so it is tested harder than anything else. The four
invariants are asserted after every single mutation, not once at the end. One
test drives sixty randomised insertions, unrankings, and reorderings, checking
all four after each. Another assigns every film a secret true ordering, answers
each comparison from it, and asserts the finished ranking reproduces that order
exactly, which verifies the insertion search is correct rather than merely
self-consistent.

Structural guarantees from the data model are tested by trying to violate them
and expecting the database to refuse: a duplicate position, an Early Access
grant with no expiry, a second live invite token, a second reaction from one
user, a season with no parent, ranking a whole series. A block overriding public
visibility is tested in both directions, since that ordering inside
can_view_profile is the easiest thing in the schema to get backwards.

Two deviations from the written data model, both deliberate:

- pgcrypto is not required. gen_random_uuid() has been core Postgres since 13,
  so the extension was dead weight.
- media_cache facets, feed event types, import statuses, and share object types
  gained check constraints. The document listed the valid values in comments;
  a constraint is the same information where it cannot rot.

Also drops a stray commit-message temp file that the scaffold commit picked up,
and gitignores it.